WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:04.200
Tiny motion sensors known as accelerometers
are at the heart of much of today's

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:09.600
technology. Built into a smartphone, for
example, the sensor detects both movement and

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.800
the angle at which the phone is being held
to trigger the rotation of the screen.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:17.940
This little chip is why a smartphone knows
when it has moved.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.260
These motion sensors are tiny but smart
because they can detect every move.

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:32.420
They start with silicon discs called Wafers.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.650
Each wafer will be used to make thousands of
motion sensors.

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:45.020
A technician aligns the discs in a cassette
using the flat part of each one as a

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:53.360
reference point. Once properly aligned, a
mechanism lowers the silicon wafers onto a

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.410
carrier made of quartz because it can
withstand intense heat.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:08.330
The technician loads the wafers into the
furnace inside 100 degrees Celsius heat and

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:11.810
steam cores, an oxide to form on the surface
of each wafer.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.750
It'll act as an electrical insulator.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:21.840
Then it's into what's known as a sputter
system.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:29.190
Here, inert gas ions bombard aluminum,
causing it to sputter onto the wafer until an

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:31.050
even layer accumulates.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.980
This aluminum layer will serve as a
conductor of electricity.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:39.960
The next machine deposits a light sensitive
chemical onto the wafer.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:45.330
The wafer spins, allowing the chemical to
flow evenly across the surface.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:52.440
This all happens under the glow of yellow
light to prevent the untimely activation of

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:57.780
the chemical which reacts to bright light
just like an unexposed photographic negative

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:05.050
would. They're now ready for that bright
light.

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:11.920
Uv light beams through tiny patterns on a
glass plate to activate the light sensitive

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:17.050
chemical. The patterns transfer to the
wafers surface, forming an outline for

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:18.850
thousands of motion sensors.

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:26.080
Another chemical then flows onto the silicon
wafer to further develop these minuscule

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:36.070
images. Next, lasers locate the wafers, flat
edge,

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:41.380
signaling a chuck to spin it into proper
position for plasma etching.

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:45.640
The etching creates thousands of free moving
3D structures.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.860
Each one is a motion sensor.

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:54.760
After etching, a sprayer blasts the surface
with carbon dioxide.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:03:01.870
It's the only way to clean it without
damaging the now movable sensors at the next

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:08.170
station. A robotic arm flips the wafer into
position, a tray moves under the robot and it

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:09.760
places the wafer on it.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:17.300
The tray retracts and loads the wafer into a
clamping system.

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:27.080
It then collects a second silicon wafer and
places it on the first one in the clamp.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:34.760
The second wafer acts as a lid to protect
the individual sensors held in the clamp.

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:38.630
A machine heat seals the two wafers and the
bond is complete.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:49.130
Next, a computerized saw cuts grooves into
the top wafer only while a steady stream of

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:50.960
water keeps the dust down.

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:56.870
The saw exposes the aluminum layer on the
bottom wafer, something that will allow each

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:59.180
sensor to make an electrical connection.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:07.980
The platform now moves the wafer a
millimeter at a time so that a probe can test

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.680
each sensor and confirm it performs
correctly.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:19.380
This continues until every sensor on the
wafer has been tested from a plain silicon

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:23.220
wafer to a unit that contains thousands of
motion sensors.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:25.980
This process has taken three weeks.

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:33.050
After the individual sensors have been cut
out and encased in plastic, they're ready for

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:34.220
a final test.

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:37.490
A robot loads each sensor into a test
socket.

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:43.730
The socket both tests the sensors
performance and programs it to customer

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:51.840
specifications. A robot packages the sensors
for shipping and once installed in phones,

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.730
tablets and computers, these motion sensors
will be ready for action.

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:02.380
If you've been to an airport, you've seen
mobile belt loaders on the tarmac.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.570
They drive from the terminal to the
aircraft, align their built in conveyor belt

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:08.500
with the entrance to the cargo hold.

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:14.190
Then load baggage or cargo on board when an
aircraft arrives, they do all that in reverse

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:16.200
to unload baggage or cargo.

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:23.490
A turn of the control handle adjusts the
height of the front of the belt loader,

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:27.690
enabling it to reach the cargo hold of even
the tallest aircraft.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:33.060
The rear height is also adjustable, so the
airport baggage handlers can set it at a

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:36.090
level comfortable for loading the vehicle.

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:40.110
And the conveyor belt are separate units in
this part of the factory.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:43.320
Workers construct the conveyors steel frame.

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:46.770
They weld together all the steel parts.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:53.310
Then grind all the weld seams flat so that
the belt will move over the frame smoothly.

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:59.050
Workers install across member just past the
midpoint of the frame.

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:04.480
It will connect with two sets of lift arms
installed in the vehicle, one which raises

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:06.340
and lowers the front of the conveyor.

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:08.950
The other which raises and lowers the rear.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:14.680
Workers insert alignment pins to position
the cross member on the frame correctly, then

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:15.970
weld it in place.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:19.200
The vehicle's chassis is also made of welded
steel.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:24.450
Workers blast the chassis and conveyor frame
with a metal abrasive.

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:30.210
This removes rust and other debris, prepping
the surface for paint to adhere properly.

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:36.960
They caulk all the welded seems to prevent
corrosion, then spray a coat of primer

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:42.150
followed by a coat of industrial grade paint
in the color the customer requested.

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:47.850
Once the paint dries, assembly can resume on
the conveyor frame.

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:49.980
They install the motor, which drives the
belt.

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:55.200
There are two options available This
hydraulic motor powered by the vehicle's

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:58.050
engine or an electric motor powered by
battery.

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:05.460
The motor rotates two large metal rollers,
one at the front of the frame, the other at

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:11.190
the rear. The belt wraps around these
rollers so that when they turn the belt,

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:19.170
moves between the end rollers running the
entire length of the conveyor.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:22.680
Workers install 58 smaller metal rollers.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:27.720
These form the floor of the conveyor and can
support up to a tonne of cargo.

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:33.270
The motor runs these rollers forward or in
reverse, depending on whether the belt is

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:35.490
loading or unloading the aircraft.

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:42.610
The belt itself is made of high strength
tyre grade rubber with an anti-skid texture

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:47.410
on the surface to be long enough to loop
around the seven and one half metre long

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:49.760
conveyor plus the end rollers.

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:52.450
The belt is 15 metres in length.

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:58.720
The ends have teeth which fit into each other
like a zipper and fasten with a metal pin.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:08.600
Elsewhere in the factory, technicians
install components on the vehicle chassis.

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:15.770
First, the front and rear axles.

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:27.340
Then on each axle, two wheels with tyres
specifically designed for long life on the

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:28.570
airport tarmac.

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:31.490
At the rear of the chassis.

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:36.230
They install the hydraulic cylinder which
raises and lowers the rear lift arms.

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:43.460
Then the vehicle's engine, which can be
either gas, diesel, propane or electric

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:49.700
powered. The vehicle drives at a maximum
speed of just 43 kilometres per hour, but

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:53.780
that's more than sufficient for traveling
between the terminal and the aircraft.

00:08:55.190 --> 00:09:00.020
After installing the power steering system,
technicians run wiring for the lights and

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:05.690
other electrical components, run hydraulic
hoses for the brakes and lift cylinders and

00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:07.700
install the controls for the conveyor.

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:13.730
They install the hydraulic cylinder, which
raises the front lift arms, then connect the

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:15.980
fluid hoses to the hydraulic pump.

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:22.550
Next, they install the front and rear lift
arms, along with the safety brace underneath

00:09:22.550 --> 00:09:25.730
to use when mechanics are working beneath
the conveyor.

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:31.010
They attach the lift arms to the front and
rear cylinders, which expand to raise the

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:33.050
arms and retract to lower them.

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:39.680
Now workers hoist the conveyor over the
vehicle and bolt the front and rear lift arms

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:41.480
to the cross member on the conveyors
underside.

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:50.870
The two units are now one mobile belt loader
ready to roll off the line and hit the

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:51.650
tarmac.

